14:36:38.492 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":118,"version":0,"year":1828,"month":4,"day":17,"sequence":5.0000,"text":"<figure>\r\n  <img src=\"images/img2842-image-map-tynemouth-to-carlow.jpg\" width=\"600px\" />\"\r\n  <figcaption>Map of journey from Tynemouth to Carlow</figcaption>\r\n</figure>","pageId":null,"type":null,"imageId":null,"marqueeId":null,"lock":null}
14:36:38.492 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1828/4/17/119
14:36:38.492 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":119,"version":0,"year":1828,"month":4,"day":17,"sequence":6.0000,"text":"<p>Left Dublin outside the Carlow coach at 9 o'clock AM. When I arrived at 4 o'clock in a state of anxiety impossible for me to describe, misery was in store for me &amp; on enquiring of the servant &quot;how my sister was?&quot; to my insufferable horror I learned she was dead. The fever was so malignant nature could not struggle with it &amp; and on Sunday last at 10 o'clock I lost one of the kindest &amp; most affectionate of sisters &amp; the town one of the best &amp; most benevolent of human beings. I was doomed not even one last parting look at her cold remains for such was the rapidity with which decomposition took place that her remains were obliged to be deposited in the select tomb next to her dear husband yesterday. However upon ...</p>","pageId":694,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":109,"lock":null}
14:36:38.492 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:38.492 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1828/4/17/119
14:36:38.492 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":119,"version":0,"year":1828,"month":4,"day":17,"sequence":6.0000,"text":"<p>Left Dublin outside the Carlow coach at 9 o'clock AM. When I arrived at 4 o'clock in a state of anxiety impossible for me to describe, misery was in store for me &amp; on enquiring of the servant &quot;how my sister was?&quot; to my insufferable horror I learned she was dead. The fever was so malignant nature could not struggle with it &amp; and on Sunday last at 10 o'clock I lost one of the kindest &amp; most affectionate of sisters &amp; the town one of the best &amp; most benevolent of human beings. I was doomed not even one last parting look at her cold remains for such was the rapidity with which decomposition took place that her remains were obliged to be deposited in the select tomb next to her dear husband yesterday. However upon ...</p>","pageId":694,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":109,"lock":null}
14:36:38.492 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1828/4/17/120
14:36:38.492 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":120,"version":0,"year":1828,"month":4,"day":17,"sequence":7.0000,"text":"<p>... upon reflection, I am resigned and convinced that I have been spared a great deal of painful feeling distress &amp; misery by not having witnessed her suffering &amp; tis a consolation to find that she has had so many kind &amp; attentive friends about her, particularly one Mr Vernon, whose conduct both to my poor sister &amp; myself I shall I trust never forget or be ungrateful for. He insists upon my staying at his house &amp; his great anxiety id to console &amp; make me comfortable. Poor little Emma has fortunately been at Captain Butlins at Broomville &amp; the boys at school. Poor children, they are too young to be sensible of the irreparable loss they have sustained. But I trust that god the father of all will spare &amp; enable me to supply the place of their dear parents they have lost. John J Baker came up to see me in the evening</p>","pageId":695,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":110,"lock":null}
14:36:38.492 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:38.493 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1828/4/17/120
14:36:38.493 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":120,"version":0,"year":1828,"month":4,"day":17,"sequence":7.0000,"text":"<p>... upon reflection, I am resigned and convinced that I have been spared a great deal of painful feeling distress &amp; misery by not having witnessed her suffering &amp; tis a consolation to find that she has had so many kind &amp; attentive friends about her, particularly one Mr Vernon, whose conduct both to my poor sister &amp; myself I shall I trust never forget or be ungrateful for. He insists upon my staying at his house &amp; his great anxiety id to console &amp; make me comfortable. Poor little Emma has fortunately been at Captain Butlins at Broomville &amp; the boys at school. Poor children, they are too young to be sensible of the irreparable loss they have sustained. But I trust that god the father of all will spare &amp; enable me to supply the place of their dear parents they have lost. John J Baker came up to see me in the evening</p>","pageId":695,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":110,"lock":null}
14:36:38.493 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1828/4/18/121
14:36:38.493 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":121,"version":0,"year":1828,"month":4,"day":18,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine day. Wind about North. Much refreshed by a good night's rest. Received a letter from William. Wrote to Mrs Scott. F Falknes &amp; Mr J Fitzmaurice called upon me. Took a long &amp; retired walk upon the banks of the river Barrow with Mr Vernon, returned to dine. John J Baker dined with us</p>","pageId":695,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":111,"lock":null}
14:36:38.493 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:38.494 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1828/4/18/121
14:36:38.494 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":121,"version":0,"year":1828,"month":4,"day":18,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine day. Wind about North. Much refreshed by a good night's rest. Received a letter from William. Wrote to Mrs Scott. F Falknes &amp; Mr J Fitzmaurice called upon me. Took a long &amp; retired walk upon the banks of the river Barrow with Mr Vernon, returned to dine. John J Baker dined with us</p>","pageId":695,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":111,"lock":null}
14:36:38.494 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1828/4/19/122
14:36:38.494 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":122,"version":0,"year":1828,"month":4,"day":19,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine morning. Rather cloudy. Wind N. Wrote to William. J Harry &amp; Mr Jamison called upon Mr I Called upon &amp; saw poor old Miss Falkman who seemed much affected &amp; grieved at dear Bopy's death. Took a walk to Clogrennane, coll Rockforts seat with Mr Vernon &amp; the boys. Caught in heavy shower of rain on our way home got wet through. The boys dined with us</p>","pageId":695,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":112,"lock":null}
14:36:38.494 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:38.494 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1828/4/19/122
14:36:38.494 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":122,"version":0,"year":1828,"month":4,"day":19,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine morning. Rather cloudy. Wind N. Wrote to William. J Harry &amp; Mr Jamison called upon Mr I Called upon &amp; saw poor old Miss Falkman who seemed much affected &amp; grieved at dear Bopy's death. Took a walk to Clogrennane, coll Rockforts seat with Mr Vernon &amp; the boys. Caught in heavy shower of rain on our way home got wet through. The boys dined with us</p>","pageId":695,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":112,"lock":null}
14:36:38.494 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1828/4/2/99
14:36:38.494 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":99,"version":0,"year":1828,"month":4,"day":2,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A very cold day with a little rain. Wind NNE. Dined on board the Hope. Had a very pleasant party.  J Tinley, H Harry &amp; Mr Marks. Received a letter from William</p>","pageId":693,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":94,"lock":null}
14:36:38.494 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:38.495 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1828/4/2/99
14:36:38.495 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":99,"version":0,"year":1828,"month":4,"day":2,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A very cold day with a little rain. Wind NNE. Dined on board the Hope. Had a very pleasant party.  J Tinley, H Harry &amp; Mr Marks. Received a letter from William</p>","pageId":693,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":94,"lock":null}
14:36:38.495 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1828/4/20/123
14:36:38.495 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":123,"version":0,"year":1828,"month":4,"day":20,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A pleasant day. Wind still from the North. Did not go to church. Read the service at home. Received a letter from William remitting a &pound;50 Bank Post bill. Took a long walk with Mr Vernon along the Athy road &amp; returned to dinner at &frac12; past 5 o'clock. The boys again with us. W W Fishbrown called upon me</p>","pageId":695,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":113,"lock":null}
14:36:38.495 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:38.495 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1828/4/20/123
14:36:38.495 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":123,"version":0,"year":1828,"month":4,"day":20,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A pleasant day. Wind still from the North. Did not go to church. Read the service at home. Received a letter from William remitting a &pound;50 Bank Post bill. Took a long walk with Mr Vernon along the Athy road &amp; returned to dinner at &frac12; past 5 o'clock. The boys again with us. W W Fishbrown called upon me</p>","pageId":695,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":113,"lock":null}
14:36:38.495 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1828/4/21/124
14:36:38.495 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":124,"version":0,"year":1828,"month":4,"day":21,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine day. Wind still from the North. Engaged all the morning in examining Dear Betsy's books &amp; papers. In the afternoon Mr Vernon, the boys &amp; I walked out to Crossleigh to see the Herrings &amp; also called upon F Falkener, went round by Old derrgy &amp; home to dinner</p>","pageId":695,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":114,"lock":null}
14:36:38.495 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:38.496 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1828/4/21/124
14:36:38.496 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":124,"version":0,"year":1828,"month":4,"day":21,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine day. Wind still from the North. Engaged all the morning in examining Dear Betsy's books &amp; papers. In the afternoon Mr Vernon, the boys &amp; I walked out to Crossleigh to see the Herrings &amp; also called upon F Falkener, went round by Old derrgy &amp; home to dinner</p>","pageId":695,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":114,"lock":null}
14:36:38.496 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1828/4/22/125
14:36:38.496 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":125,"version":0,"year":1828,"month":4,"day":22,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A continuance of the same sort of weather with the cool wind. Engaged all the morning with Mr Turner in examining &amp; entering rents. In the afternoon Mr Vernon &amp; I took a walk along the Kilkenny Road, returned home to drefs &amp; took a family dinner with Mr W<sup>en</sup> Fishburn who was exceedingly kind, but yet I should have preferred dining quietly at Mr Vernon's. Received a letter from William</p>","pageId":695,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":115,"lock":null}
14:36:38.496 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
