14:36:38.706 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":559,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":16,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Got up a little after 7 o'clock. Had a fresh breeze all night from the southward. The ship making 8 knots per hour. A continuance of most beautiful weather. At 12 o'clock took observation Lat:57.4 long: 7.17 having   made 101 miles in the last 24 hours, making 284 since our departure. Employed part of the morning in painting the skiff. At near 2 o'clock saw land on our starboard bow. Off Jutland. The wind, northeast fair, is now against ...</p>","pageId":739,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":537,"lock":null}
14:36:38.706 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/16/560
14:36:38.706 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":560,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":16,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... SE &amp; <span class=highlight_yellow>us</span>. The wind getting to SSE took in studding sails &amp; stood <span class=highlight_yellow>aft</span> of to Norway coast which we saw between 4 &amp; 5 o'clock. Had our fishing lines out all this morning but only caught two gurnets.</p>\r\n<p>A little before 5 o'clock seeing it very black to windward &amp; a squall coming, stowed <span class=highlight_yellow>t</span><span class=highlight_yellow>o</span><span class=highlight_yellow>p</span><span class=highlight_yellow> </span><span class=highlight_yellow>galla</span><span class=highlight_yellow>n</span><span class=highlight_yellow>d</span> sails &amp; lowered topsails upon the <span class=highlight_yellow>Cep</span> just as the squall took us, very heavy, but little rain. It lasted about &frac14; of an hour. Got topsails hoisted again single reefed. Wind seemed much inclined to change. At one time we had it to our great delight from the westward where we squared our yards, but to our inexpressible annoyance had scarcely done when it returned to the old quarter.</p>\r\n<p>During this evening a large fleet of loaded vessels upwards of 80 sail from the Baltic passed us as we let out both reefs. Walked the deck until 10 o'clock when we had our glass of comfort &amp; turned in. Little wind from about the south</p>","pageId":740,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":538,"lock":null}
14:36:38.706 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:38.706 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/16/560
14:36:38.706 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":560,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":16,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... SE &amp; <span class=highlight_yellow>us</span>. The wind getting to SSE took in studding sails &amp; stood <span class=highlight_yellow>aft</span> of to Norway coast which we saw between 4 &amp; 5 o'clock. Had our fishing lines out all this morning but only caught two gurnets.</p>\r\n<p>A little before 5 o'clock seeing it very black to windward &amp; a squall coming, stowed <span class=highlight_yellow>t</span><span class=highlight_yellow>o</span><span class=highlight_yellow>p</span><span class=highlight_yellow> </span><span class=highlight_yellow>galla</span><span class=highlight_yellow>n</span><span class=highlight_yellow>d</span> sails &amp; lowered topsails upon the <span class=highlight_yellow>Cep</span> just as the squall took us, very heavy, but little rain. It lasted about &frac14; of an hour. Got topsails hoisted again single reefed. Wind seemed much inclined to change. At one time we had it to our great delight from the westward where we squared our yards, but to our inexpressible annoyance had scarcely done when it returned to the old quarter.</p>\r\n<p>During this evening a large fleet of loaded vessels upwards of 80 sail from the Baltic passed us as we let out both reefs. Walked the deck until 10 o'clock when we had our glass of comfort &amp; turned in. Little wind from about the south</p>","pageId":740,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":538,"lock":null}
14:36:38.706 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/17/561
14:36:38.706 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":561,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":17,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>After a regular good &amp; sound snooze, turned out at &frac12; past 7 o'clock.</p>\r\n<p>During the night slight shower with very little wind. To our great joy when we looked up through the cabin sky light, we saw the yards square &amp; studding sails set. Wind WNW. After breakfast &amp; commenced a heavy rain, which continued until 12 o'clock. Saw the <span class=highlight_yellow>Shaw</span> light at 2 o'clock.</p>\r\n<p>Employed myself in painting the ship's name upon the skiff &amp; the carpenter finishing painting the underside. Bob Marsh being too lazy to complete his work.</p>\r\n<p>Wind continuing favourable &amp; a fine fresh breeze going 5 or 6 knots. At 9 o'clock saw the <span class=highlight_yellow>Trindlew</span> &amp; the island of <span class=highlight_yellow>Lcefsoe</span> bearing NW by W, distant 5 or 6 miles.</p>\r\n<p>Took our warm glass of brandy &amp; water &amp; got to bed before 10 o'clock. <span class=highlight_yellow>Looking</span> the sea evidently rising.</p>","pageId":740,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":539,"lock":null}
14:36:38.706 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:38.707 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/17/561
14:36:38.707 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":561,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":17,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>After a regular good &amp; sound snooze, turned out at &frac12; past 7 o'clock.</p>\r\n<p>During the night slight shower with very little wind. To our great joy when we looked up through the cabin sky light, we saw the yards square &amp; studding sails set. Wind WNW. After breakfast &amp; commenced a heavy rain, which continued until 12 o'clock. Saw the <span class=highlight_yellow>Shaw</span> light at 2 o'clock.</p>\r\n<p>Employed myself in painting the ship's name upon the skiff &amp; the carpenter finishing painting the underside. Bob Marsh being too lazy to complete his work.</p>\r\n<p>Wind continuing favourable &amp; a fine fresh breeze going 5 or 6 knots. At 9 o'clock saw the <span class=highlight_yellow>Trindlew</span> &amp; the island of <span class=highlight_yellow>Lcefsoe</span> bearing NW by W, distant 5 or 6 miles.</p>\r\n<p>Took our warm glass of brandy &amp; water &amp; got to bed before 10 o'clock. <span class=highlight_yellow>Looking</span> the sea evidently rising.</p>","pageId":740,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":539,"lock":null}
14:36:38.707 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/18/562
14:36:38.707 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":562,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":18,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Turned out about 8 o'clock. This morning after a rather restless night. At 11 o'clock last night it began to blow heavy with sea making the wind suddenly changed to the SE much to our disappointment &amp; increased to a regular squall. Studding sails taken in &amp; top gallent sails stowed. Between 12 &amp; 1 o'clock am Bob &amp; I not being able to sleep, from the motion of the ship &amp; precarious articles of furniture tumbling about the cabin, clothed ourselves &amp; went upon deck. Then about day break found the people employed in double reefing <span class=highlight_yellow>the</span> topsail &amp; taking one reef in the main-top-sail blowing tremendously hard but clear weather. A little after 2 o'clock saw the sun rise which amply <span class=highlight_yellow>r</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ewarded</span> us from our want of rest, a grand &amp; magnificent sight. This morning particularly so. Old sol seemed to have an immediate influence over the wind &amp; waves, for from the time he made his appearance, they gradually lulled. Before 3 o'clock, Bob &amp; I again turned in &amp; had a sound snooze until 8 o'clock.</p>\r\n<p>The master informed us that at 6 o'clock it being almost calm. The wind again suddenly changed to the SW but although fair for us for the present, we make but little way, there being a current &amp; hard sea against us. It appeared there are no tides here &amp; in the Baltic &amp; which ever way the wind is, the current sets. Before we went to our roost again at 3 o'clock we saw the <span class=highlight_yellow>Riddigen</span> lighthouse on the Norway coast, beaming <span class=highlight_yellow>mainly</span> SE, distant about 5 miles according to my calculation. Before breakfast set top gallent sail &amp; let out the reefs of topsails. At 10 o'clock the wind got to the westward. In the afternoon set front-top-mast studding sail. It about 5 o'clock the wind getting from the NNW ...</p>","pageId":740,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":540,"lock":null}
14:36:38.707 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
