14:36:38.721 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2308,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":30,"sequence":3.0000,"text":"<p>... some prayer. Returned to the ship about 11 o'clock. Had a glass of cool brandy &amp; water &amp; went to bed</p>","pageId":747,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2248,"lock":null}
14:36:38.721 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/30/582
14:36:38.721 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":582,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":30,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Got up at 8 o'clock. A beautiful morning &amp; exceptionally hot. Found a great many Shields ships laying in the mole amongst them the Gordeson, Cholmley, the Wellington, H Daeghton &amp; &amp; Bob Marsh &amp; I went on board &amp; had our breakfast with H Deyghtow &amp; his wife. The master &amp; J Galbraith went on shore early to Mr Booker to report the ship &amp; <span class=highlight_yellow>vansack</span></p>","pageId":746,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":557,"lock":null}
14:36:38.721 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:38.721 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/30/582
14:36:38.721 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":582,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":30,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Got up at 8 o'clock. A beautiful morning &amp; exceptionally hot. Found a great many Shields ships laying in the mole amongst them the Gordeson, Cholmley, the Wellington, H Daeghton &amp; &amp; Bob Marsh &amp; I went on board &amp; had our breakfast with H Deyghtow &amp; his wife. The master &amp; J Galbraith went on shore early to Mr Booker to report the ship &amp; <span class=highlight_yellow>vansack</span></p>","pageId":746,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":557,"lock":null}
14:36:38.721 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/30/583
14:36:38.721 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":583,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":30,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... all necessary business. Bob &amp; I remained on board all day, expecting the collector every hour to search the ship &amp; independently of which the heat was so great that we did not feel much <span class=highlight_yellow>despaired</span> to move. We got an awning  <span class=highlight_yellow>aerown</span> over the main boom &amp; kept ourselves as cool as we could. The collector did not come on board until 6 o'clock in the evening, a pompous, haughty Russian &amp; like all the rest, lazy &amp; will not move an inch out of the way to <span class=highlight_yellow>seron</span> anyone. I find there is a very bad feeling at present towards the English. He or rather his officers searched the ship, but put his seals upon the hatches again. <span class=highlight_yellow>F</span><span class=highlight_yellow>or</span> packages he did not search, only counted them &amp; ordered his officers  on board not to take them off till the morning when people might deliver the ballast.</p>\r\n<p>We again had a stroll about the town &amp; ramparts.  <font color=#BFBFBF>The </font>home<font color=#BFBFBF>s</font> of the people are very cleanly, though certainly some, such as the shopkeepers are more so than I expected. But the labouring classes are a dirty, lousy surplus, leftover &amp; miserable set of slaves, little removed from beastly. In every shop there is a panting or <span class=highlight_yellow>fr</span><span class=highlight_yellow>e</span><span class=highlight_yellow>sc</span><span class=highlight_yellow>o</span> of our saviour or the virgin &amp; in various parks of the town were erections in which was placed what they intended I suppose to be representations of our saviour &amp; the virgin or some saint decked out in a most ridiculous style with <span class=highlight_yellow>linsel</span>. They were the most hideous likenesses to the human face that <span class=highlight_yellow>there</span> could be. Before each was a <span class=highlight_yellow>candle</span> which was kept constantly burning &amp; <span class=highlight_yellow>how</span> every one of the poor ignorant devils on payment stopped crossing themselves, bowing &amp; seeming to repeat ...</p>","pageId":747,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":558,"lock":null}
