14:36:38.737 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":618,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":17,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A wet morning, but by the time we had breakfast, it had cleared up. Paid our account for lodgings etc &amp; got on board the steam boat at 9 o'clock &amp; proceeded to Cronstad, where we ...</p>","pageId":758,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":582,"lock":null}
14:36:38.737 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/17/619
14:36:38.737 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":619,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":17,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... arrived before 12 o'clock. Had to take our luggage again to the Customs House to be searched. A mere matter of form, for they only opened the boxes without turning the things over &amp; after a little trouble &amp; delay, we were allowed to carry all on board, accompanied by an officer. Employed ourselves during this afternoon in stowing our traps away &amp; putting everything in order.</p>\r\n<p>After tea the captain, Marsh &amp; I went on shore, called at the butchers &amp; baker &amp; spent the remainder of the evening at a friend's where we had some wine. Got on board again between 11 &amp; 12 o'clock.</p>\r\n<p>Taken 250 casks of tallow on board</p>","pageId":759,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":583,"lock":null}
14:36:38.737 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:38.738 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/17/619
14:36:38.738 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":619,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":17,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... arrived before 12 o'clock. Had to take our luggage again to the Customs House to be searched. A mere matter of form, for they only opened the boxes without turning the things over &amp; after a little trouble &amp; delay, we were allowed to carry all on board, accompanied by an officer. Employed ourselves during this afternoon in stowing our traps away &amp; putting everything in order.</p>\r\n<p>After tea the captain, Marsh &amp; I went on shore, called at the butchers &amp; baker &amp; spent the remainder of the evening at a friend's where we had some wine. Got on board again between 11 &amp; 12 o'clock.</p>\r\n<p>Taken 250 casks of tallow on board</p>","pageId":759,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":583,"lock":null}
14:36:38.738 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/18/620
14:36:38.738 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":620,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":18,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Another lighter came down last night with the remainder of our tallow.</p>\r\n<p>After breakfast, Bob, JG &amp; I went on shore to order some boots etc. Met with Mr Bartholomew. Went with him &amp; had our dinner on board the David. He left by the steam boat for Petersburg at 5 o'clock. Bab &amp; I went with Stephenson to the Butchers to see the beef salted &amp; afterwards took a stroll.</p>\r\n<p>Got in 120 casks of tallow this day.</p>","pageId":759,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":584,"lock":null}
14:36:38.738 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:38.738 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/18/620
14:36:38.738 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":620,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":18,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Another lighter came down last night with the remainder of our tallow.</p>\r\n<p>After breakfast, Bob, JG &amp; I went on shore to order some boots etc. Met with Mr Bartholomew. Went with him &amp; had our dinner on board the David. He left by the steam boat for Petersburg at 5 o'clock. Bab &amp; I went with Stephenson to the Butchers to see the beef salted &amp; afterwards took a stroll.</p>\r\n<p>Got in 120 casks of tallow this day.</p>","pageId":759,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":584,"lock":null}
14:36:38.738 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/19/621
14:36:38.738 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":621,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":19,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A rainy morning. Cleared up about 10 o'clock. Dressed ourselves &amp; went on shore to the English Church, just in time to hear the communion service &amp; sermon. Dined on board at 1 o'clock. Had a good sea pie &amp; some roast mutton, which enjoyed much. In the afternoon went on shore, excepting Bob, took some mead to cool us, a very agreeable &amp; refreshing drink made of honey &amp; ginger, not unlike ginger wine. Looked at the <span class=highlight_yellow>Neger</span> market, a very curious and busy scene, crowded with people bargaining &amp; selling. A great many sailors there, regular jacks, very amusing to hear them. Went in the evening to the gardens, which are open to the public on a Sunday. The walks are very prettily laid out &amp; shaded with young trees. A great many of the fashionables of Cronstat where promenading &amp; an excellent military band played for about a couple of hours.</p>\r\n<p>Got on board early</p>","pageId":759,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":585,"lock":null}
14:36:38.738 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:38.739 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/19/621
14:36:38.739 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":621,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":19,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A rainy morning. Cleared up about 10 o'clock. Dressed ourselves &amp; went on shore to the English Church, just in time to hear the communion service &amp; sermon. Dined on board at 1 o'clock. Had a good sea pie &amp; some roast mutton, which enjoyed much. In the afternoon went on shore, excepting Bob, took some mead to cool us, a very agreeable &amp; refreshing drink made of honey &amp; ginger, not unlike ginger wine. Looked at the <span class=highlight_yellow>Neger</span> market, a very curious and busy scene, crowded with people bargaining &amp; selling. A great many sailors there, regular jacks, very amusing to hear them. Went in the evening to the gardens, which are open to the public on a Sunday. The walks are very prettily laid out &amp; shaded with young trees. A great many of the fashionables of Cronstat where promenading &amp; an excellent military band played for about a couple of hours.</p>\r\n<p>Got on board early</p>","pageId":759,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":585,"lock":null}
14:36:38.739 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/2/2309
14:36:38.739 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2309,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":2,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... trip. On board we met Mr Fordeson who was also going up. Our papers were taken from us by an officer, who it appeared gave it to the master of the boat &amp; his duty I understand is to deliver them to some officer in Petersburg. We had a very pleasant passage <span class=highlight_yellow>aft</span> of three houses &amp; landed about a mile below the city at the wharf of a Mr ____ a Scotchman &amp; proprietor of the steam boats. He was the first who started steam boats in Russia under an agreement with the Government that for a certain number of years no other boats were to be allowed to oppose him. He has thus accomplished a large fortune.</p>\r\n<p>Taking a <span class=highlight_yellow>donkey &amp; cart</span> for our luggage we drove to a Mrs Welson, a widow who keeps a boarding house in that part of the town called Backline. Here we found <font color=#BFBFBF>a </font>room &amp; after lunching, Bob &amp; I strolled out to endeavour &amp; find out the gentleman to whom we had letters of  introduction. The first person we called upon was a Mr Dye, a friend of In<font color=#BFBFBF>spect</font>o<font color=#BFBFBF>r</font>  Hodgson whom we found a very pleasant gentlemanly fellow residing in the first line, upon the Island &amp; in very elegant quarters. He gave us an invitation to dinner for tomorrow.</p>\r\n<p>We were struck with the magnificence of everything we saw. It not possible to summarize the splendour of the streets &amp; various buildings you see on every side as you pass along every house is upon a large &amp; noble scale &amp; whichever way your turn your eyes ...</p>","pageId":748,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2249,"lock":null}
