14:36:38.747 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":633,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":29,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<figure>\r\n  <img src=\"images/img2910-peregrine-pickle-1st-edition.png\" width=\"220px\" />\r\n  <figcaption>Peregrine Pickle 1st edition</figcaption>\r\n</figure>","pageId":null,"type":null,"imageId":null,"marqueeId":null,"lock":null}
14:36:38.747 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/29/634
14:36:38.747 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":634,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":29,"sequence":3.0000,"text":"<p>Turned out this morning at &frac12; past 7 o'clock. Weather changed being quite calm, the alterations took place last night at 12 o'clock with heavy rain, thunder &amp; lightning, which &amp; I did not hear being on a very comfortable <font color=#BFBFBF>birth </font>&amp; sound asleep. In the course of the morning, when off Hanger Head, the wind got to the north (at 2 o'clock wind east) enabling us again to set studding sails, steering a WSW course.</p>\r\n<p>Amused myself all day in reading Roderick Random and Peregrine Pickle. Turned in at 11 o'clock after having our grog. Little wind.</p>","pageId":761,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":596,"lock":null}
14:36:38.747 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:38.747 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/29/634
14:36:38.747 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":634,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":29,"sequence":3.0000,"text":"<p>Turned out this morning at &frac12; past 7 o'clock. Weather changed being quite calm, the alterations took place last night at 12 o'clock with heavy rain, thunder &amp; lightning, which &amp; I did not hear being on a very comfortable <font color=#BFBFBF>birth </font>&amp; sound asleep. In the course of the morning, when off Hanger Head, the wind got to the north (at 2 o'clock wind east) enabling us again to set studding sails, steering a WSW course.</p>\r\n<p>Amused myself all day in reading Roderick Random and Peregrine Pickle. Turned in at 11 o'clock after having our grog. Little wind.</p>","pageId":761,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":596,"lock":null}
14:36:38.747 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/3/588
14:36:38.747 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":588,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":3,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Turned out this morning a little before 8 o'clock after a sound slump. But still excessively tired. The weather horrendously hot, staid quietly in the house until 1 o'clock. Accompanied Bob marsh to call upon Mr Wishaw, introduced to him. Very polite. Then walked over to the Island &amp; called upon Changes. Not liking our quarters with Mrs Wilson &amp; having to pay 8 rubles per day whether we dined there or not, Bob &amp; I passed the time until Change  houses in looking for other lodgings where we could have a bed &amp; breakfast &amp; be at liberty to do dining, but without success, not being able to speak the language &amp; having no person with us that could understand.</p>\r\n<p>Went to change where we met Mr Dye &amp; afterwards walked home with him to dinner. His partners Mr Lumley &amp; a Mr Leidsey from Leeds dined with us. He gave us a very good feed &amp; moderate quantities of wine &amp; at 8 o'clock got into a boat &amp; rowed across the <span class=highlight_yellow>R</span><span class=highlight_yellow>i</span><span class=highlight_yellow>v</span><span class=highlight_yellow>er</span> to the island ... </p>","pageId":749,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":563,"lock":null}
14:36:38.747 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:38.748 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/3/588
14:36:38.748 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":588,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":3,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Turned out this morning a little before 8 o'clock after a sound slump. But still excessively tired. The weather horrendously hot, staid quietly in the house until 1 o'clock. Accompanied Bob marsh to call upon Mr Wishaw, introduced to him. Very polite. Then walked over to the Island &amp; called upon Changes. Not liking our quarters with Mrs Wilson &amp; having to pay 8 rubles per day whether we dined there or not, Bob &amp; I passed the time until Change  houses in looking for other lodgings where we could have a bed &amp; breakfast &amp; be at liberty to do dining, but without success, not being able to speak the language &amp; having no person with us that could understand.</p>\r\n<p>Went to change where we met Mr Dye &amp; afterwards walked home with him to dinner. His partners Mr Lumley &amp; a Mr Leidsey from Leeds dined with us. He gave us a very good feed &amp; moderate quantities of wine &amp; at 8 o'clock got into a boat &amp; rowed across the <span class=highlight_yellow>R</span><span class=highlight_yellow>i</span><span class=highlight_yellow>v</span><span class=highlight_yellow>er</span> to the island ... </p>","pageId":749,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":563,"lock":null}
14:36:38.748 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/30/635
14:36:38.748 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":635,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":30,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Turned out before 8 o'clock. A fine morning. A light breeze at ENE going about 3 or 3 &frac12; knots. Studding sails set. About dinner time it began to rain &amp; continued very heavy until 3 o'clock. When we passed round Dago Island &amp; entered the Baltic. At &frac12; past 5 o'clock the wind changed to west ...</p>","pageId":761,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":597,"lock":null}
14:36:38.748 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:38.748 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/30/635
14:36:38.748 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":635,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":30,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Turned out before 8 o'clock. A fine morning. A light breeze at ENE going about 3 or 3 &frac12; knots. Studding sails set. About dinner time it began to rain &amp; continued very heavy until 3 o'clock. When we passed round Dago Island &amp; entered the Baltic. At &frac12; past 5 o'clock the wind changed to west ...</p>","pageId":761,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":597,"lock":null}
14:36:38.748 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/30/636
14:36:38.748 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":636,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":30,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>In the evening light winds &amp; inclined to calm. Turned in before 10 o'clock after a glass of grog</p>","pageId":762,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":598,"lock":null}
14:36:38.748 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:38.749 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/30/636
14:36:38.749 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":636,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":30,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>In the evening light winds &amp; inclined to calm. Turned in before 10 o'clock after a glass of grog</p>","pageId":762,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":598,"lock":null}
14:36:38.749 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/31/637
14:36:38.749 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":637,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":31,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Fine weather and light breezes from WNW. During the whole of the morning mainly calm, barely going 2 knots with rather a strong sea. In the evening about 5 o'clock the wind got to the ENE &amp; increased to a fine breeze. The ship going about 4 &amp; 5 knots &amp; from midnight to 4 o'clock the wind blew so fresh she increased her rate to 6 knots. This fine breeze continued all night.</p>","pageId":762,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":599,"lock":null}
14:36:38.749 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:38.749 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/31/637
14:36:38.749 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":637,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":31,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Fine weather and light breezes from WNW. During the whole of the morning mainly calm, barely going 2 knots with rather a strong sea. In the evening about 5 o'clock the wind got to the ENE &amp; increased to a fine breeze. The ship going about 4 &amp; 5 knots &amp; from midnight to 4 o'clock the wind blew so fresh she increased her rate to 6 knots. This fine breeze continued all night.</p>","pageId":762,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":599,"lock":null}
14:36:38.749 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/4/589
14:36:38.749 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":589,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":4,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Another fine day &amp; continuance of hot weather. Bob &amp; I after breakfast again sallied out in search of lodgings &amp; were fortunate enough to see Mr Lindsay. A the wonder of his quarter. The house in kept by a yorkshireman of the name of Webster, a horse dealer. Here we succeeded in getting two rooms at 2 rubbles a night, in the larger one which I propose taking, having a small bed in for John G<font color=#BFBFBF>abraith</font> my bed is concealed by a large screen which when drawn round it, leaves the other part of the room for us &amp; answers the purposes of a breakfast parlour.</p>\r\n<p>J Galbraith arrived  this morning by the steam boat &amp; Mr Webster brought him up to the lodgings. Bob &amp; I went to the office &amp; got our passports returned. We afterwards accompanied Mr Lendsay who got our names put down at the Club formed of English &amp; German merchants much upon the same plan as the clubs in London, tis a large building, formally the residence of a nobleman upon whom the Quay principally occupied by English merchants. The suit of rooms very splendid &amp; most elegantly furnished. Here after changing, we dined &amp; always intended doing so when not otherwise engaged. The company being Saturday, was not numerous, but we sat down to a very handsome dinner which I understand cost about 2 &frac12; rubbles</p>","pageId":750,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":564,"lock":null}
14:36:38.749 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:38.750 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/4/589
14:36:38.750 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":589,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":4,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Another fine day &amp; continuance of hot weather. Bob &amp; I after breakfast again sallied out in search of lodgings &amp; were fortunate enough to see Mr Lindsay. A the wonder of his quarter. The house in kept by a yorkshireman of the name of Webster, a horse dealer. Here we succeeded in getting two rooms at 2 rubbles a night, in the larger one which I propose taking, having a small bed in for John G<font color=#BFBFBF>abraith</font> my bed is concealed by a large screen which when drawn round it, leaves the other part of the room for us &amp; answers the purposes of a breakfast parlour.</p>\r\n<p>J Galbraith arrived  this morning by the steam boat &amp; Mr Webster brought him up to the lodgings. Bob &amp; I went to the office &amp; got our passports returned. We afterwards accompanied Mr Lendsay who got our names put down at the Club formed of English &amp; German merchants much upon the same plan as the clubs in London, tis a large building, formally the residence of a nobleman upon whom the Quay principally occupied by English merchants. The suit of rooms very splendid &amp; most elegantly furnished. Here after changing, we dined &amp; always intended doing so when not otherwise engaged. The company being Saturday, was not numerous, but we sat down to a very handsome dinner which I understand cost about 2 &frac12; rubbles</p>","pageId":750,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":564,"lock":null}
14:36:38.750 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/4/590
14:36:38.750 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":590,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":4,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... a head, exclusive of wine.</p>\r\n<p>In the evening we took a stroll. On our return to our lodgings, rather tired, &amp; thirsty, finished a bottle of beer, a couple of glasses of grog &amp; went to bed.</p>","pageId":751,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":565,"lock":null}
14:36:38.750 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:38.750 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/4/590
14:36:38.750 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":590,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":4,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... a head, exclusive of wine.</p>\r\n<p>In the evening we took a stroll. On our return to our lodgings, rather tired, &amp; thirsty, finished a bottle of beer, a couple of glasses of grog &amp; went to bed.</p>","pageId":751,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":565,"lock":null}
14:36:38.750 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/5/2311
14:36:38.750 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2311,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":5,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... long there as it threatened another storm, we therefor got into our boat, but  had not been long on board before the rain came down in torrents with most awful  thunder &amp; lightning. It however cleared up again before we reached our landing place. These gardens are about two miles or more from Petersburg &amp; where the people flocked to in thousands on a Sunday. The walks upon this &amp; neighbouring islands are beautiful. We fortunately had a boat with an awning over it.</p>\r\n<p>On our return took a glass of grog &amp; went to bed</p>","pageId":751,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2251,"lock":null}
14:36:38.750 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:38.751 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/5/2311
14:36:38.751 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2311,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":5,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... long there as it threatened another storm, we therefor got into our boat, but  had not been long on board before the rain came down in torrents with most awful  thunder &amp; lightning. It however cleared up again before we reached our landing place. These gardens are about two miles or more from Petersburg &amp; where the people flocked to in thousands on a Sunday. The walks upon this &amp; neighbouring islands are beautiful. We fortunately had a boat with an awning over it.</p>\r\n<p>On our return took a glass of grog &amp; went to bed</p>","pageId":751,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2251,"lock":null}
14:36:38.751 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/5/591
14:36:38.751 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":591,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":5,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Got up about 8 o'clock after a good night's rest in very comfortable &amp; clean beds.</p>\r\n<p>At 11 o'clock went to the English Church belonging to the E: Factory. The duty done by Mr Law, brother of Lord Ellenbro &amp; Chaplin to the Embassy. This church is next to the clubhouse &amp; the interior extremely elegant &amp; neat. The exterior has the appearance of a private dwelling distinguishable from the rest by a statue of Hope on the top</p>\r\n<p>Dined at the lodgings at 4 o'clock that we might get to the gardens of <span class=highlight_yellow>Kres</span><span class=highlight_yellow>tovskiy</span> after dinner &amp; went to the gardens. On our way caught in a horrendous shower of rain with thunder and lightning. The gardens not very crowded no doubt in consequence of the wet, which also prevented the people dancing &amp; us of a great deal of amusement. There were their bands, one military in the gardens, but the best was in front of the master of the Hunt House, and a horn band in a boat. This band is composed of a great number of men &amp; each plays his separate note having a horn of one key, it is the finest thing I ever heard &amp; had a delightful effect upon the water. We did not stay...</p>","pageId":751,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":566,"lock":null}
14:36:38.751 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:38.751 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/5/591
14:36:38.751 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":591,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":5,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Got up about 8 o'clock after a good night's rest in very comfortable &amp; clean beds.</p>\r\n<p>At 11 o'clock went to the English Church belonging to the E: Factory. The duty done by Mr Law, brother of Lord Ellenbro &amp; Chaplin to the Embassy. This church is next to the clubhouse &amp; the interior extremely elegant &amp; neat. The exterior has the appearance of a private dwelling distinguishable from the rest by a statue of Hope on the top</p>\r\n<p>Dined at the lodgings at 4 o'clock that we might get to the gardens of <span class=highlight_yellow>Kres</span><span class=highlight_yellow>tovskiy</span> after dinner &amp; went to the gardens. On our way caught in a horrendous shower of rain with thunder and lightning. The gardens not very crowded no doubt in consequence of the wet, which also prevented the people dancing &amp; us of a great deal of amusement. There were their bands, one military in the gardens, but the best was in front of the master of the Hunt House, and a horn band in a boat. This band is composed of a great number of men &amp; each plays his separate note having a horn of one key, it is the finest thing I ever heard &amp; had a delightful effect upon the water. We did not stay...</p>","pageId":751,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":566,"lock":null}
14:36:38.751 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/6/592
14:36:38.751 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":592,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":6,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A showery morning, but cleared up towards the middle of the day. We set off with the intention of hearing the service at the Casen Church, but loitering on the way, were too late. The church however was open being a holyday &amp; most splendid &amp; gorgeous it is. The paintings of the different saints etc. are very well done &amp; the most magnificent pillars of granite, highly <span class=highlight_yellow>polished</span>, that can be imagined, each if one stone, there are about 100 of them. Here are deposited flags &amp; keys of towns taken from the enemy at what was no little gratification to our pride, not an English colour amongst them. After a long stroll, we returned to res for dinner. Dined at the club. A very small party. Principally gentlemen. Left the table immediately after dinner &amp; walked about the remainder of the evening. Had our grog &amp; went to bed at 11 o'clock.</p>","pageId":751,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":567,"lock":null}
14:36:38.751 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:38.752 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/6/592
14:36:38.752 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":592,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":6,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A showery morning, but cleared up towards the middle of the day. We set off with the intention of hearing the service at the Casen Church, but loitering on the way, were too late. The church however was open being a holyday &amp; most splendid &amp; gorgeous it is. The paintings of the different saints etc. are very well done &amp; the most magnificent pillars of granite, highly <span class=highlight_yellow>polished</span>, that can be imagined, each if one stone, there are about 100 of them. Here are deposited flags &amp; keys of towns taken from the enemy at what was no little gratification to our pride, not an English colour amongst them. After a long stroll, we returned to res for dinner. Dined at the club. A very small party. Principally gentlemen. Left the table immediately after dinner &amp; walked about the remainder of the evening. Had our grog &amp; went to bed at 11 o'clock.</p>","pageId":751,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":567,"lock":null}
14:36:38.752 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/7/2312
14:36:38.752 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2312,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":7,"sequence":6.0000,"text":"<p>... city of Petersburg, it contains only three very small rooms, something after this plan <font color=#00B0F0>[See figure]</font>  Here is also to be see the boat which he built himself. How it is <span class=highlight_yellow>made</span> known he was an excellent carpenter &amp; used to row in. In the Citadel church there are some beautiful things in ivory of his turning &amp; workmanship.</p>\r\n<p>Dined with Mr Gillibrand only his wife &amp; Mr Lindsey there. Spent a very quiet day &amp; left early being foreign port day, which over the water &amp; took tea with a friend of Mr Lumleys &amp; Bob &amp; I afterwards had a stroll down the Mews by Prospect to see the people &amp; illuminations for the Emperors birthday. They don't as with us place candle &amp; light in the windows etc, but <span class=highlight_yellow>feet</span> what they call <span class=highlight_yellow>fat pots</span> along the street upon the edge of the pavement &amp; most horrible <span class=highlight_yellow>effluence</span> they send forth. We got to bed about 12 o'clock</p>","pageId":752,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2252,"lock":null}
14:36:38.752 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
