eId":754,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2254,"lock":null}
14:36:38.758 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/9/603
14:36:38.758 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":603,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":9,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fair morning. Mr Linsey, his friend, Bob &amp; I started at 8 o'clock in a <span class=highlight_yellow>barrack</span> for the Imperial Palace of <span class=highlight_yellow>Sarskoi</span> &amp; <span class=highlight_yellow>Pavhousky</span>, about 22 <span class=highlight_yellow>versts</span> off, where we arrived in about 2 &frac12; hours. <span class=highlight_yellow>Sarskoi</span> is a summer palace &amp; surpasses everything we have yet seen for brilliance &amp; splendour. It is grand &amp; magnificent beyond the possibility of just description. Every room is most richly guild with the finest gold and I showed surprise at an immense expenditure at one time. The roof &amp; all the exterior ornaments were done in the same way, but &lsquo;tis said that Catherine when much in want of money, made an agreement with a rich Jew, who advanced her a certain sum, for the purpose of stripping the gold off the exterior. Nothing can exceed the attention of the Chapple. The <span class=highlight_yellow>furniture</span> is exceedingly rich &amp; handsome &amp; great taste is displayed ...</p>","pageId":753,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":571,"lock":null}
14:36:38.758 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:38.759 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/9/603
14:36:38.759 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":603,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":9,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fair morning. Mr Linsey, his friend, Bob &amp; I started at 8 o'clock in a <span class=highlight_yellow>barrack</span> for the Imperial Palace of <span class=highlight_yellow>Sarskoi</span> &amp; <span class=highlight_yellow>Pavhousky</span>, about 22 <span class=highlight_yellow>versts</span> off, where we arrived in about 2 &frac12; hours. <span class=highlight_yellow>Sarskoi</span> is a summer palace &amp; surpasses everything we have yet seen for brilliance &amp; splendour. It is grand &amp; magnificent beyond the possibility of just description. Every room is most richly guild with the finest gold and I showed surprise at an immense expenditure at one time. The roof &amp; all the exterior ornaments were done in the same way, but &lsquo;tis said that Catherine when much in want of money, made an agreement with a rich Jew, who advanced her a certain sum, for the purpose of stripping the gold off the exterior. Nothing can exceed the attention of the Chapple. The <span class=highlight_yellow>furniture</span> is exceedingly rich &amp; handsome &amp; great taste is displayed ...</p>","pageId":753,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":571,"lock":null}
14:36:38.759 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/9/604
14:36:38.759 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":604,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":9,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... throughout. The grand dining room is a noble apartments &amp; of great size and there is great comfort neatness &amp; cleanliness in the Emperor &amp; Empress's private apartments. The grounds which are very extensive being about the diameter of five miles, are laid out with great taste &amp; judgement &amp; kept in high order &amp; beautifully interspaces with wood &amp; water. In the plantations is a summer or private dining room built by Catherine &amp; elaborated by her making it the scene of her private armoires. In the centre of the building, upon the finest floor is the dining room &amp; that <span class=highlight_yellow>night</span> not having the attendance of servants, the table was worked by machinery &amp; upon a bell being rung, the whole descended to a room below &amp; there replenished, or if merely a clean plate was wanted, the plate stand, alone went down. But of each corner of the dining room was a cabinet intended no doubt for beds or couches. But when we were there, the whole was under repair &amp; consequently not furnished.</p>\r\n<p>The Palace of Jarskoi not being large enough for the whole of the family, another upon a smaller &amp; less magnificent scale was built in the grounds. A plain but handsome mansion. There are also a number of houses at a short distance from the Palace, built after the Chinese fashion forming a small village ...</p>","pageId":754,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":572,"lock":null}
14:36:38.759 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:38.759 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/9/604
14:36:38.759 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":604,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":9,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... throughout. The grand dining room is a noble apartments &amp; of great size and there is great comfort neatness &amp; cleanliness in the Emperor &amp; Empress's private apartments. The grounds which are very extensive being about the diameter of five miles, are laid out with great taste &amp; judgement &amp; kept in high order &amp; beautifully interspaces with wood &amp; water. In the plantations is a summer or private dining room built by Catherine &amp; elaborated by her making it the scene of her private armoires. In the centre of the building, upon the finest floor is the dining room &amp; that <span class=highlight_yellow>night</span> not having the attendance of servants, the table was worked by machinery &amp; upon a bell being rung, the whole descended to a room below &amp; there replenished, or if merely a clean plate was wanted, the plate stand, alone went down. But of each corner of the dining room was a cabinet intended no doubt for beds or couches. But when we were there, the whole was under repair &amp; consequently not furnished.</p>\r\n<p>The Palace of Jarskoi not being large enough for the whole of the family, another upon a smaller &amp; less magnificent scale was built in the grounds. A plain but handsome mansion. There are also a number of houses at a short distance from the Palace, built after the Chinese fashion forming a small village ...</p>","pageId":754,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":572,"lock":null}
14:36:38.759 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/1/638
14:36:38.759 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":638,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":1,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Got up at &frac12; past 7 o'clock. A fine morning. After a few hours rain  &amp; a beautiful breeze the whole of the night. At 8 o'clock am the wind changed to SE obliged us to get in lower studding sails. At 2 o'clock the wind got to S by W strong breezes &amp; hazy weather &amp; rain, very cold, more like November than August. At 6 o'clock close in with Gothland Ostergarmholm light house N &frac12; W distant about 12 miles. Tacked ship blowing fresh with a heavy head sea. Between 9 &amp; 10 o'clock discussed our grog &amp; turned in.</p>\r\n<p>This afternoon the Dido of Hull captain ____ passed astern of us. We hoisted our colours &amp; he answered</p>","pageId":762,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":600,"lock":null}
14:36:38.759 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:38.760 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/1/638
14:36:38.760 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":638,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":1,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Got up at &frac12; past 7 o'clock. A fine morning. After a few hours rain  &amp; a beautiful breeze the whole of the night. At 8 o'clock am the wind changed to SE obliged us to get in lower studding sails. At 2 o'clock the wind got to S by W strong breezes &amp; hazy weather &amp; rain, very cold, more like November than August. At 6 o'clock close in with Gothland Ostergarmholm light house N &frac12; W distant about 12 miles. Tacked ship blowing fresh with a heavy head sea. Between 9 &amp; 10 o'clock discussed our grog &amp; turned in.</p>\r\n<p>This afternoon the Dido of Hull captain ____ passed astern of us. We hoisted our colours &amp; he answered</p>","pageId":762,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":600,"lock":null}
14:36:38.760 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/10/647
14:36:38.760 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":647,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":10,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Clear weather but blowing very fresh from WSW. Obliged to reef &amp; double reef the topsails &amp; slow the trysails. Could make no way against both wind &amp; current. Lat: by obs 58.2 N</p>","pageId":764,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":609,"lock":null}
