14:36:38.767 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2322,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":20,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... get at them. Bob &amp; I much annoyed having made certain of having a comfortable dinner &amp; bed on show &amp; talked over our plans of future proceedings. He having determined to stay a few days at Shields &amp; take his passage in some other vessel.</p>\r\n<p>The wind &amp; sea being right aft, we soon passed Huntcliff foot again &amp; at 8 o'clock left Flamborough Head bearing west distant 2 or 3 miles.</p>\r\n<p>A great many light ships in Burlington Bat as we passed.</p>\r\n<p>Ship made a great deal of water during the day</p>","pageId":767,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2262,"lock":null}
14:36:38.767 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/20/660
14:36:38.767 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":660,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":20,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Turned out this morning between 5 &amp; 6 o'clock found the ship turning to windward off Marsden Rock blowing hard from N by E. The wind having increased during the night with a high sea. At about 9 o'clock, the breeze increasing &amp; no chance of us reaching Tynemouth of getting on shore. Even if we had, we bore away for London much to our chagrin &amp; disappointment having had Shields Dockwray Square <span class=highlight_yellow>etc</span> in view &amp; not able to ...</p>","pageId":767,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":622,"lock":null}
14:36:38.767 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:38.768 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/20/660
14:36:38.768 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":660,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":20,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Turned out this morning between 5 &amp; 6 o'clock found the ship turning to windward off Marsden Rock blowing hard from N by E. The wind having increased during the night with a high sea. At about 9 o'clock, the breeze increasing &amp; no chance of us reaching Tynemouth of getting on shore. Even if we had, we bore away for London much to our chagrin &amp; disappointment having had Shields Dockwray Square <span class=highlight_yellow>etc</span> in view &amp; not able to ...</p>","pageId":767,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":622,"lock":null}
14:36:38.768 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/21/661
14:36:38.768 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":661,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":21,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Got up at &frac12; past 7 o'clock. A fine morning. Most moderate weather. Wind N by W night after Studdington set Just passed the Floating light, having made good way during the night. Had a good night's rest, but much plagued with the rheumatism</p>\r\n<p>Passed Cromer at 1 o'clock with less wind Between 8 &amp; 9 o'clock in the evening abreast of Harborough endeavouring to get through the gateway, but falling calm were driven back by the tide. With much trouble got the ship head round to the westward &amp; a light breeze springing up, stood a crop to the land. At 10 o'clock went to bed. Tide turned &amp; the captain intending to work up to Winterton &amp; anchor, the wind being from the SW.</p>","pageId":767,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":623,"lock":null}
14:36:38.768 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:38.769 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/21/661
14:36:38.769 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":661,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":21,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Got up at &frac12; past 7 o'clock. A fine morning. Most moderate weather. Wind N by W night after Studdington set Just passed the Floating light, having made good way during the night. Had a good night's rest, but much plagued with the rheumatism</p>\r\n<p>Passed Cromer at 1 o'clock with less wind Between 8 &amp; 9 o'clock in the evening abreast of Harborough endeavouring to get through the gateway, but falling calm were driven back by the tide. With much trouble got the ship head round to the westward &amp; a light breeze springing up, stood a crop to the land. At 10 o'clock went to bed. Tide turned &amp; the captain intending to work up to Winterton &amp; anchor, the wind being from the SW.</p>","pageId":767,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":623,"lock":null}
14:36:38.769 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/22/2323
14:36:38.769 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2323,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":22,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... struggled hard to prevent it escaping. I felt that &quot;Richard was himself again&quot; &amp; no longer the prospect for the present of ruin &amp; a jail before me. At 4 o'clock pm we brought up abreast of the Navy Hospital, within a short distance of the shore, blowing a heavy gale from the SW &amp; rejoiced that we had got into such a snug roadstead, instead of being at sea at night</p>\r\n<p>Such tempestuous &amp; unsettled weather for the time of year I don't remember. This is the fourth gale we have experienced since leaving Elseneur &amp; although it is such a short distance to the river, I almost despair of reaching without some disaster for bad luck seems to thwart us, whichever way we steer.</p>\r\n<p>Bob &amp; I turned in a little after 8 o'clock. The gale rather encased with rain</p>","pageId":768,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2263,"lock":null}
14:36:38.769 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:38.769 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/22/2323
14:36:38.769 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2323,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":22,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... struggled hard to prevent it escaping. I felt that &quot;Richard was himself again&quot; &amp; no longer the prospect for the present of ruin &amp; a jail before me. At 4 o'clock pm we brought up abreast of the Navy Hospital, within a short distance of the shore, blowing a heavy gale from the SW &amp; rejoiced that we had got into such a snug roadstead, instead of being at sea at night</p>\r\n<p>Such tempestuous &amp; unsettled weather for the time of year I don't remember. This is the fourth gale we have experienced since leaving Elseneur &amp; although it is such a short distance to the river, I almost despair of reaching without some disaster for bad luck seems to thwart us, whichever way we steer.</p>\r\n<p>Bob &amp; I turned in a little after 8 o'clock. The gale rather encased with rain</p>","pageId":768,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2263,"lock":null}
14:36:38.769 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/22/662
14:36:38.769 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":662,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":22,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Went upon deck at 8 o'clock. Found the ship outside the sands nearly as far as Lowestoft turning to windward with the wind at WSW. It seems that last night soon after we turned in, the wind suddenly got to the W &amp; WNW where they squared the yards, set studdingsails &amp; ran out of harbour gateway.</p>\r\n<p>The wind increasing from the WNW stowed topgallant sails, reefed topsails, afterwards stowed square mainmast &amp; bore away &amp; at 12 o'clock when off Southwold, bore away for Yarmouth, passing through St Nicholas Gateway, where to my inexpressible horror and alarm we nearly struck upon the north end of the Croton Sand, the captain, me, taking the last buoy for the buoy of a wreck in the rocks. The lead was thrown. The mate called out &frac12; 3 then &frac14; 3 &ndash; 3 but when it came to &frac14; less 3 my state fear &amp; agitation, for the safety of the ship, was not to be envied, expecting every moment she would strike &amp; had she, the least that could have happened would most likely have been to deliver the cargo &amp; repair, being already very leaky, but she probably might have gone down in deep water or have drifted on the sand to leeward where the mast probably have been lost. However the next throw of the lead the mate called &frac14; 3 &amp; then &frac12; &amp; 4 fathoms was a wonderful relief to me. Such was agitation that I could almost have cried indeed I wouldn't say that the big tear did not stand in my eye but I ...</p>","pageId":768,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":624,"lock":null}
14:36:38.769 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:38.770 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/22/662
14:36:38.770 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":662,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":22,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Went upon deck at 8 o'clock. Found the ship outside the sands nearly as far as Lowestoft turning to windward with the wind at WSW. It seems that last night soon after we turned in, the wind suddenly got to the W &amp; WNW where they squared the yards, set studdingsails &amp; ran out of harbour gateway.</p>\r\n<p>The wind increasing from the WNW stowed topgallant sails, reefed topsails, afterwards stowed square mainmast &amp; bore away &amp; at 12 o'clock when off Southwold, bore away for Yarmouth, passing through St Nicholas Gateway, where to my inexpressible horror and alarm we nearly struck upon the north end of the Croton Sand, the captain, me, taking the last buoy for the buoy of a wreck in the rocks. The lead was thrown. The mate called out &frac12; 3 then &frac14; 3 &ndash; 3 but when it came to &frac14; less 3 my state fear &amp; agitation, for the safety of the ship, was not to be envied, expecting every moment she would strike &amp; had she, the least that could have happened would most likely have been to deliver the cargo &amp; repair, being already very leaky, but she probably might have gone down in deep water or have drifted on the sand to leeward where the mast probably have been lost. However the next throw of the lead the mate called &frac14; 3 &amp; then &frac12; &amp; 4 fathoms was a wonderful relief to me. Such was agitation that I could almost have cried indeed I wouldn't say that the big tear did not stand in my eye but I ...</p>","pageId":768,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":624,"lock":null}
14:36:38.770 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/23/663
14:36:38.770 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":663,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":23,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Turned out after a food nights rest at 8 o'clock. The gale as heavy as ever with rain. (Sent top gallant yards driven &amp; struck the mast)</p>\r\n<p>At 11 o'clock finer weather &amp; some appearance of clearing up having had a glimpse of the sun.</p>\r\n<p>Wrote a letter to William &amp; Mr Clark of the Duke of Clarence coming on board. We all went on shore at 12 o'clock to put the letter into the post. Walked about &amp; saw a little of the town of Yarmouth with which I was much pleased. After having some bread &amp; cheese, <span class=highlight_yellow>portion</span> &amp; a glass of grog, we left the jetty about 4 o'clock ...</p>","pageId":768,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":625,"lock":null}
14:36:38.770 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:38.770 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/23/663
14:36:38.770 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":663,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":23,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Turned out after a food nights rest at 8 o'clock. The gale as heavy as ever with rain. (Sent top gallant yards driven &amp; struck the mast)</p>\r\n<p>At 11 o'clock finer weather &amp; some appearance of clearing up having had a glimpse of the sun.</p>\r\n<p>Wrote a letter to William &amp; Mr Clark of the Duke of Clarence coming on board. We all went on shore at 12 o'clock to put the letter into the post. Walked about &amp; saw a little of the town of Yarmouth with which I was much pleased. After having some bread &amp; cheese, <span class=highlight_yellow>portion</span> &amp; a glass of grog, we left the jetty about 4 o'clock ...</p>","pageId":768,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":625,"lock":null}
14:36:38.770 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/23/664
14:36:38.770 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":664,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":23,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... reached the ship in about &frac12; a hour after a heavy pull. Still blowing very hard. Mr Clark took tea with us in the evening.  The weather much improved both sea &amp; wind having fallen.</p>","pageId":769,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":626,"lock":null}
14:36:38.770 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
