14:36:38.777 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":673,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":30,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Got up much refreshed. More moderate weather. Wind NE rather cloudy. We fully intended to have gone to church, but Bob &amp; I took up so much linin in the washing &amp; cleaning ourselves that it was past the hour before we were aware of it. Turned out a very fine day. We called upon Mr Ward who had gone to Paris will William. Walked through St James park up to Hyde Park where we strolled till 6 o'clock. Very few people or carriages there. Town exceedingly thin from thence came we went to Mr Piazza Covent garden &amp; enjoyed a comfortable dinner &amp; a good bottle of old port. Returned to Chambers between 10 &amp; 11 o'clock &amp; went to bed.</p>","pageId":771,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":635,"lock":null}
14:36:38.777 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/31/674
14:36:38.777 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":674,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":31,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A cloudy morning, rather cool. Wind NE. Walked into the city. Called at the office, nothing there. Proceeded after being at Chapmans to St Catherine's docks. The ship got safe in yesterday. Stephenson noted a protest this morning, the ship having been so leaky. Found Mrs Stephenson on board, eat some dinner with them. Attended <span class=highlight_yellow>cha</span><span class=highlight_yellow>mb</span><span class=highlight_yellow>er</span><span class=highlight_yellow>s</span>. Walked westward &amp; called upon Jobling.</p>","pageId":771,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":636,"lock":null}
14:36:38.777 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:38.777 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/31/674
14:36:38.777 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":674,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":31,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A cloudy morning, rather cool. Wind NE. Walked into the city. Called at the office, nothing there. Proceeded after being at Chapmans to St Catherine's docks. The ship got safe in yesterday. Stephenson noted a protest this morning, the ship having been so leaky. Found Mrs Stephenson on board, eat some dinner with them. Attended <span class=highlight_yellow>cha</span><span class=highlight_yellow>mb</span><span class=highlight_yellow>er</span><span class=highlight_yellow>s</span>. Walked westward &amp; called upon Jobling.</p>","pageId":771,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":636,"lock":null}
14:36:38.777 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/4/641
14:36:38.777 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":641,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":4,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A delightful change. Now beautiful weather. Wind NW &amp; W. Light breeze &amp; sea very much fallen, only going about 2 knots. At 12 o'clock by observation at lat:55.47N. Employed myself all the morning in preparing the wood for a dog kennel. At 2 o'clock pm the wind got to the WSW course NW. At 5 pm set studding sails, top masts &amp; main top gallant. Sea quite fallen &amp; a fine fresh breeze at 6 o'clock wind S course W by S going 5 knots at 10 o'clock <span class=highlight_yellow>discharged</span> our grog &amp; turned in for the night, ship going 5 &frac12; knots</p>","pageId":763,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":603,"lock":null}
14:36:38.777 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:38.778 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/4/641
14:36:38.778 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":641,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":4,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A delightful change. Now beautiful weather. Wind NW &amp; W. Light breeze &amp; sea very much fallen, only going about 2 knots. At 12 o'clock by observation at lat:55.47N. Employed myself all the morning in preparing the wood for a dog kennel. At 2 o'clock pm the wind got to the WSW course NW. At 5 pm set studding sails, top masts &amp; main top gallant. Sea quite fallen &amp; a fine fresh breeze at 6 o'clock wind S course W by S going 5 knots at 10 o'clock <span class=highlight_yellow>discharged</span> our grog &amp; turned in for the night, ship going 5 &frac12; knots</p>","pageId":763,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":603,"lock":null}
14:36:38.778 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/5/2319
14:36:38.778 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2319,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":5,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... not inhabited. They are all within a stone's throw of each other. After passing Ertholmene, we had a most delightful sail under the Island of Bornholm &amp; a good view of it. This is one of the richest and most beautiful islands that can be imagined. It is in fact a perfect garden &amp; to us had all the appearance of a gentleman's park. The soil is good &amp; exceedingly productive yielding as much in one season as would keep the inhabitants several years. A boat came out to us from the shore &amp; we purchased of them four ducks &frac34; score of eggs &amp; 2 pounds of butter for which we gave only 6/6</p>\r\n<p>When we reached the west end of the island then wind took us all at a <span class=highlight_yellow>lk</span>, but soon favoured us again. In the evening it became quite calm. At &frac12; past 8 o'clock the captain, Bob &amp; I as usual had our grog &amp; being in the humour, <span class=highlight_yellow>had</span> some supper &amp; had a regular jollification, blew a cloud &amp; got to roost between 12 &amp; 1 o'clock</p>","pageId":763,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2259,"lock":null}
14:36:38.778 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:38.778 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/5/2319
14:36:38.778 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2319,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":5,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... not inhabited. They are all within a stone's throw of each other. After passing Ertholmene, we had a most delightful sail under the Island of Bornholm &amp; a good view of it. This is one of the richest and most beautiful islands that can be imagined. It is in fact a perfect garden &amp; to us had all the appearance of a gentleman's park. The soil is good &amp; exceedingly productive yielding as much in one season as would keep the inhabitants several years. A boat came out to us from the shore &amp; we purchased of them four ducks &frac34; score of eggs &amp; 2 pounds of butter for which we gave only 6/6</p>\r\n<p>When we reached the west end of the island then wind took us all at a <span class=highlight_yellow>lk</span>, but soon favoured us again. In the evening it became quite calm. At &frac12; past 8 o'clock the captain, Bob &amp; I as usual had our grog &amp; being in the humour, <span class=highlight_yellow>had</span> some supper &amp; had a regular jollification, blew a cloud &amp; got to roost between 12 &amp; 1 o'clock</p>","pageId":763,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2259,"lock":null}
14:36:38.778 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/5/642
14:36:38.778 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":642,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":5,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Another beautiful day. The ship making good way about 4 &frac12; knots with a fine breeze at SSE going free. We can perceive the Islands of <span class=highlight_yellow>E</span><span class=highlight_yellow>r</span><span class=highlight_yellow>tholm</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ene</span> &amp; Bornholm with the glass. The ship much to my annoyance very leaky. Obliged to pump every hour. At 1 o'clock pm passed close to <span class=highlight_yellow>Erth</span><span class=highlight_yellow>o</span><span class=highlight_yellow>lm</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ene</span>. With a fine breeze at S by E. Studding sails set. These are small rocky islands belonging to Denmark about 11 miles from Bornholm. Upon the largest is a lighthouse, a few small batteries &amp; houses. On the south side a very good harbour I understand. The other two islands are mostly rocks &amp; ...</p>","pageId":763,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":604,"lock":null}
