14:36:38.897 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":912,"version":0,"year":1830,"month":4,"day":13,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... party consisting of the <span class=highlight_yellow>Joblary</span> &amp; he took them all away, which prevented the remainder of the company, being too few, having any dancing. Craig it appears took the party with much of the gentlemen to his own house &amp; prevailing upon the musicians to accompany him. May these had a dance. But that would be expected from a fellow brought up behind a counter ! the filles ! place for him.</p>","pageId":812,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":864,"lock":null}
14:36:38.897 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1830/4/14/913
14:36:38.897 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":913,"version":0,"year":1830,"month":4,"day":14,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A beautiful spring morning. Wind West. Walked with Williams to Preston &amp; looked at the different tenements &amp; ordered what repairs were necessary afterwards we  crofsid the conatrg &amp; called upon Mr Flbceght, Whitley Park when this it began to rain&amp; we got wet through before we reached home &amp; dinner at &frac12; past 2 o'clock Mary Carp left us &amp; returned to Newcastle with A Deighlow. In the evening at the Duke of York with A Harry &amp; Mr Heweson</p>","pageId":812,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":865,"lock":null}
