14:36:41.521 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/2313
14:36:41.521 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2313,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":8,"sequence":6.0000,"text":"<p>... the <span class=highlight_yellow>Pete</span><span class=highlight_yellow>rs</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ho</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ff</span> Road, but being plagued with my toothache &amp; my jaw much swollen, I was obliged to let Bob go by himself &amp; dined at the club with Mr Lindsey. He &amp; I in the evening each got a horse of Me Webster who keeps excellent cattle &amp; took a very pleasant ride down by <span class=highlight_yellow>Catharinoff</span> &amp; round home by the <span class=highlight_yellow>Petershoff</span> Road. After having our grog, retired to bed between 11 &amp; 12 o'clock, exceedingly stiff &amp; fatigued.</p>","pageId":753,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2253,"lock":null}
14:36:41.521 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:41.521 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/2313
14:36:41.521 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2313,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":8,"sequence":6.0000,"text":"<p>... the <span class=highlight_yellow>Pete</span><span class=highlight_yellow>rs</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ho</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ff</span> Road, but being plagued with my toothache &amp; my jaw much swollen, I was obliged to let Bob go by himself &amp; dined at the club with Mr Lindsey. He &amp; I in the evening each got a horse of Me Webster who keeps excellent cattle &amp; took a very pleasant ride down by <span class=highlight_yellow>Catharinoff</span> &amp; round home by the <span class=highlight_yellow>Petershoff</span> Road. After having our grog, retired to bed between 11 &amp; 12 o'clock, exceedingly stiff &amp; fatigued.</p>","pageId":753,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2253,"lock":null}
14:36:41.521 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/2314
14:36:41.521 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2314,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":9,"sequence":3.0000,"text":"<p>... intended for the foreign ambassadors when the court is there.</p>\r\n<p>In the grounds we took lunch &amp; at the same time had the pleasure of being most unmercifully bit by mosquitoes.</p>\r\n<p>From this we drove to Parlousky another Palace the residence of the late <span class=highlight_yellow>Dowagn</span> Empress the widow of Paul &amp; mother of the present Emperor. It is a handsome building but nothing splendid in its appearance. The interior we did not see. The grounds though not as extensive, I think more beautiful than Jarskoi. There is more variety of scenery, hill &amp; dale nature has dome much for it. The other is <span class=highlight_yellow>obliged</span> to ask. In these grounds are some very fine statues &amp; near the Palace is one of the most splendidly executed things I ever saw, these female figures upon a pedestal in marble as large as life, <span class=highlight_yellow>supposing</span> an <span class=highlight_yellow>usn</span> a <span class=highlight_yellow>ne</span><span class=highlight_yellow> </span><span class=highlight_yellow>plas</span><span class=highlight_yellow> </span><span class=highlight_yellow>lettra</span><span class=highlight_yellow> of statuary</span>.</p>\r\n<p>After taking some brandy &amp; water at the top of the waterfall to quench our thirst, we again got into the carriage &amp; drove homewards, stopping at a fine fountain on the road where we dined, having carried what <span class=highlight_yellow>prog</span> &amp; drinkables we wanted with us.</p>\r\n<p>When at Jarskoi we saw the Emperor's cows, pastures and fine animals &amp; the breeds of all nations. This establishment the late Emperor Alexander took great delight in, and it is kept up at considerable extent. The cows are dressed the same as horses &amp; in fine condition.</p>","pageId":754,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2254,"lock":null}
14:36:41.521 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:41.522 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/2314
14:36:41.522 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2314,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":9,"sequence":3.0000,"text":"<p>... intended for the foreign ambassadors when the court is there.</p>\r\n<p>In the grounds we took lunch &amp; at the same time had the pleasure of being most unmercifully bit by mosquitoes.</p>\r\n<p>From this we drove to Parlousky another Palace the residence of the late <span class=highlight_yellow>Dowagn</span> Empress the widow of Paul &amp; mother of the present Emperor. It is a handsome building but nothing splendid in its appearance. The interior we did not see. The grounds though not as extensive, I think more beautiful than Jarskoi. There is more variety of scenery, hill &amp; dale nature has dome much for it. The other is <span class=highlight_yellow>obliged</span> to ask. In these grounds are some very fine statues &amp; near the Palace is one of the most splendidly executed things I ever saw, these female figures upon a pedestal in marble as large as life, <span class=highlight_yellow>supposing</span> an <span class=highlight_yellow>usn</span> a <span class=highlight_yellow>ne</span><span class=highlight_yellow> </span><span class=highlight_yellow>plas</span><span class=highlight_yellow> </span><span class=highlight_yellow>lettra</span><span class=highlight_yellow> of statuary</span>.</p>\r\n<p>After taking some brandy &amp; water at the top of the waterfall to quench our thirst, we again got into the carriage &amp; drove homewards, stopping at a fine fountain on the road where we dined, having carried what <span class=highlight_yellow>prog</span> &amp; drinkables we wanted with us.</p>\r\n<p>When at Jarskoi we saw the Emperor's cows, pastures and fine animals &amp; the breeds of all nations. This establishment the late Emperor Alexander took great delight in, and it is kept up at considerable extent. The cows are dressed the same as horses &amp; in fine condition.</p>","pageId":754,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2254,"lock":null}
14:36:41.522 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/2315
14:36:41.522 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2315,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":11,"sequence":3.0000,"text":"<p>... took a walk in the summer gardens of the Palace. On our return had a glass of grog with Mr Lindsey &amp; got to bed at 12 o'clock.</p>\r\n<p>Got admission into the premises where they are building the new Isaac Church &amp; now engaged in erecting the granite pillars for the Portico &amp; they use of an immense size most beautifully polished. The finest &amp; longest in the world, brought from the quarries in Finland, their circumference about 30 feet &amp; height upwards of 50 &amp; although they weight ___ tons, they raise them in 20 minutes</p>","pageId":755,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2255,"lock":null}
14:36:41.522 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:41.522 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/2315
14:36:41.522 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2315,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":11,"sequence":3.0000,"text":"<p>... took a walk in the summer gardens of the Palace. On our return had a glass of grog with Mr Lindsey &amp; got to bed at 12 o'clock.</p>\r\n<p>Got admission into the premises where they are building the new Isaac Church &amp; now engaged in erecting the granite pillars for the Portico &amp; they use of an immense size most beautifully polished. The finest &amp; longest in the world, brought from the quarries in Finland, their circumference about 30 feet &amp; height upwards of 50 &amp; although they weight ___ tons, they raise them in 20 minutes</p>","pageId":755,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2255,"lock":null}
14:36:41.522 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/2316
14:36:41.522 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2316,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":15,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... filled with ancient armour, arms &amp; belonging to various nations and exceedingly curious, colours &amp; standards taken battle, models of fortifications, cannon &amp; mortars, the cloths, arms &amp; accoutrements belonging to peter the Great, the late Emperor Alexander &amp; several Generals etc. etc. etc. all very curious &amp; well worth seeing, particularly the funeral car for the late Alexander, a most magnificent vehicle.</p>\r\n<p>After leaving the Arsenal Me Lindsley accompanied us to the market near the Cesan Church where we purchased several articles as presents for the Lees etc.</p>\r\n<p>Came home to dinner, Mr Lindsley with us had <span class=highlight_yellow>Sadaek</span> for dinner, <span class=highlight_yellow>these</span> fish and another called <span class=highlight_yellow>Saggee</span> are fine flavoured &amp; most excellent.</p>\r\n<p>The pay of the Russian soldiers &amp; officers is very trifling. That of a <span class=highlight_yellow>S</span><span class=highlight_yellow>aba</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ll</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ere</span> &pound;16 a year &amp; a private 11 Rubbles, not much more than 9/- English. The private of the bodyguard have 13 Rubbles</p>\r\n<p>Being rather tired in the evening we only took a short walk down the Newsky Prospect</p>","pageId":757,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2256,"lock":null}
14:36:41.522 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:41.523 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/2316
14:36:41.523 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2316,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":15,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... filled with ancient armour, arms &amp; belonging to various nations and exceedingly curious, colours &amp; standards taken battle, models of fortifications, cannon &amp; mortars, the cloths, arms &amp; accoutrements belonging to peter the Great, the late Emperor Alexander &amp; several Generals etc. etc. etc. all very curious &amp; well worth seeing, particularly the funeral car for the late Alexander, a most magnificent vehicle.</p>\r\n<p>After leaving the Arsenal Me Lindsley accompanied us to the market near the Cesan Church where we purchased several articles as presents for the Lees etc.</p>\r\n<p>Came home to dinner, Mr Lindsley with us had <span class=highlight_yellow>Sadaek</span> for dinner, <span class=highlight_yellow>these</span> fish and another called <span class=highlight_yellow>Saggee</span> are fine flavoured &amp; most excellent.</p>\r\n<p>The pay of the Russian soldiers &amp; officers is very trifling. That of a <span class=highlight_yellow>S</span><span class=highlight_yellow>aba</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ll</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ere</span> &pound;16 a year &amp; a private 11 Rubbles, not much more than 9/- English. The private of the bodyguard have 13 Rubbles</p>\r\n<p>Being rather tired in the evening we only took a short walk down the Newsky Prospect</p>","pageId":757,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2256,"lock":null}
14:36:41.523 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/2317
14:36:41.523 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2317,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":16,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>.. in the vapour bath, which we looked into, but that was all for it was, so intolerably hot  we were glad to draw back, others afterwards having part oiled themselves &amp; perspiring most copiously at every pore were employed scouring &amp; pumping upon each other with cold water, all of course en buff. We next took the liberty of paying the ladies a visit, whom we saw the same way employed &amp; in the same state of nudity. Their moderately poor <span class=highlight_yellow>innocents</span> did not make them shrink at all from our scrutinising gaze. Indeed they seemed to enjoy the fun as much as we did. I think there were from 30 to 40 of them. We didist not venture to look into the ladies vapour bath, for we could not get to without passing through ducking <span class=highlight_yellow>guard</span> &amp; we were afraid they might favour us with a sousing.</p>\r\n<p>We then proceeded to the market &amp; purchased several pairs of slippers etc &amp; returned home by the summer gardens. Took a couple of glasses of iced grog &amp; went to bed</p>","pageId":758,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2257,"lock":null}
14:36:41.523 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
14:36:41.523 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/2317
14:36:41.523 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2317,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":16,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>.. in the vapour bath, which we looked into, but that was all for it was, so intolerably hot  we were glad to draw back, others afterwards having part oiled themselves &amp; perspiring most copiously at every pore were employed scouring &amp; pumping upon each other with cold water, all of course en buff. We next took the liberty of paying the ladies a visit, whom we saw the same way employed &amp; in the same state of nudity. Their moderately poor <span class=highlight_yellow>innocents</span> did not make them shrink at all from our scrutinising gaze. Indeed they seemed to enjoy the fun as much as we did. I think there were from 30 to 40 of them. We didist not venture to look into the ladies vapour bath, for we could not get to without passing through ducking <span class=highlight_yellow>guard</span> &amp; we were afraid they might favour us with a sousing.</p>\r\n<p>We then proceeded to the market &amp; purchased several pairs of slippers etc &amp; returned home by the summer gardens. Took a couple of glasses of iced grog &amp; went to bed</p>","pageId":758,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2257,"lock":null}
14:36:41.523 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/2318
14:36:41.523 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2318,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":23,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... humour to go on shore. Obliged to go to bed. The pain &amp; <span class=highlight_yellow>opium</span> making me exceptionally drowsy. Bought some flannel &amp; hartshorn in the evening which I put upon my face when I went to bed</p>","pageId":760,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2258,"lock":null}
14:36:41.523 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
