14:36:41.545 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":266,"version":0,"year":1828,"month":9,"day":4,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A beautiful day. Wind NE. Changed to NW &amp; N with thunder &amp; lightning &amp; apparently heavy rain in Hampshire, but returned to the old quarter at night. F Barclay left us this morning for London. Sorry to loose him, a pleasant fellow. Mr Lee &amp; Laura in the chaise &amp; the rest walked to Binstead to see the abbey of which there is little remaining, after lunching on the grass with difficulty &amp; getting our feet covered with mud. We returned home by the beach.</p>","pageId":708,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":250,"lock":null}
14:36:41.545 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/267
14:36:41.545 [Test worker] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":267,"version":0,"year":1828,"month":9,"day":5,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Delightful weather. Wind SE. Sea quite calm. Captain &amp; Mrs Lewis, Bob Marsh &amp; I took a boat &amp; amused ourselves four hours fishing. Great success having caught 89 what they call whiting <span class=highlight_yellow>pacets</span>, a small fish. Took a walk on the pier after dressing &amp; dined at Navarino. The two young Wainwrights dined with us</p>","pageId":708,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":251,"lock":null}
